+# UsdtYieldVault — pre-deployment review
+
+**Verdict: do not ship this on Monday.**
+
+I found eight problems. Two of them are blockers in the literal sense — the first
+means the contract cannot take a single deposit on mainnet, the second means it
+cannot ever pay the yield it is named after. A third lets the owner take ~98% of
+any incoming deposit. None of these are visible to the existing suite, because the
+existing suite only ever talks to `MockUSDT` (a textbook OpenZeppelin ERC20) and
+`MockAavePool` (which pays no interest). 39/39 green and 100% line coverage are
+both true and both uninformative here: coverage measures which lines ran, not
+which of your assumptions were tested. The two mocks encode exactly the two
+assumptions that are wrong.
+
+`src/` is untouched. `test/UsdtYieldVault.t.sol` and `test/mocks/` are untouched.
+Everything below lives in new files:
+
+| File | What it is |
+| --- | --- |
+| `test/fork/RealUsdtDeposit.t.sol` | Mainnet fork, real USDT + real Aave V3 pool |
+| `test/fork/StrandedYield.t.sol` | Mainnet fork, real Aave interest accrual |
+| `test/exploit/VaultExploits.t.sol` | Offline, uses your mocks unmodified |
+| `test/exploit/VaultInvariants.t.sol` | Offline invariant/fuzz suite |
+| `test/replica/UsdtReplica.sol` | Behavioural replica of the deployed TetherToken |
+| `test/replica/UsdtReplica.t.sol` | Offline twin of the fork findings |
+
+## How to re-run everything
+
+```bash
+export ETH_RPC_URL=https://ethereum-rpc.publicnode.com
+forge test --match-path 'test/fork/*'
+forge test --match-path 'test/exploit/*'
+forge test --match-path 'test/replica/*'
+```
+
+The tests are written as **assertions of properties a depositor would assume hold**.
+They fail. That failure is the finding. Nothing here passes by construction.
+
+Two caveats so you are not surprised when you re-run:
+
+* The fork tests run against the **latest** block, not a pinned one. The free RPC
+  endpoints reachable from this machine (`publicnode`, `flashbots`, `1rpc`) all
+  refuse archive state, so `createSelectFork(url, blockNumber)` fails with
+  `state at block #N is pruned`. The consequence is that the *numbers* in the
+  yield finding drift a little between runs as the Aave USDT supply rate moves.
+  The *failures* do not drift — they are structural.
+* `test/exploit/VaultInvariants.t.sol` **passes**, on purpose. It is the part of
+  the review that came back clean, and it is reported as such in section 9.
+
+---
+
+## F1 — CRITICAL — `deposit()` reverts on real USDT. The vault is inert on mainnet.
+
+`src/UsdtYieldVault.sol:94`:
+
+```solidity
+usdt.approve(address(aavePool), amount);
+```
+
+`usdt` is typed `IERC20`, so the compiler emits a call that expects 32 bytes of
+return data and decodes them as a `bool`. The USDT at
+`0xdAC17F958D2ee523a2206206994597C13D831ec7` is the pre-EIP-20-final TetherToken:
+its `approve` is declared `returns ()` and returns **nothing**. Decoding an empty
+returndata buffer reverts, and takes the whole deposit with it.
+
+Note the asymmetry in the contract's own code: line 93 routes `transferFrom`
+through `SafeERC20.safeTransferFrom`, which tolerates the missing return value.
+Line 94 does not use SafeERC20 at all. A one-line inconsistency that happens to be
+fatal.
+
+`MockUSDT` inherits OpenZeppelin's `ERC20`, whose `approve` returns `true`, so no
+test in the existing suite can see this.
+
