+## F3 — CRITICAL — the owner can sandwich a deposit and keep ~98% of it.
+
+`setDepositFee` has no upper bound, no timelock, and takes effect in the same
+block:
+
+```solidity
+function setDepositFee(uint256 newFeeBps) external onlyOwner {
+    depositFeeBps = newFeeBps;          // no bound check
+}
+```
+
+and `deposit` credits the depositor with shares for `netAmount` while supplying —
+and booking — the full `amount`:
+
+```solidity
+uint256 fee = (amount * depositFeeBps) / BPS_DENOMINATOR;
+uint256 netAmount = amount - fee;
+shares = convertToShares(netAmount);   // shares for the net
+...
+totalAssetsStored += amount;           // assets for the gross
+```
+
+The difference is pure dilution in favour of whoever already holds shares. If the
+owner holds shares, the owner is that beneficiary. The owner front-runs a pending
+deposit with `setDepositFee(9900)`, lets it land, back-runs with
+`setDepositFee(0)` so the next victim sees an innocent fee, and then exits.
+
+This needs no compromised key and no bug — it is the documented owner privilege
+used as intended. `test_SetDepositFeeUpdatesValue` in the existing suite asserts
+the setter works and stops there.
+
