+## F4 — HIGH — `pause()` locks depositors in, and can be made permanent.
+
+`withdraw` carries `whenNotPaused` (line 107). A pause is meant to stop new money
+entering a system under stress; here it also stops existing money leaving. Worse,
+`Ownable.renounceOwnership()` is inherited and un-overridden, so the owner can
+pause and then renounce, leaving no address on earth able to call `unpause()`.
+Every depositor is locked out of their funds permanently.
+
+This is reachable by accident — a routine "renounce ownership to decentralise"
+step taken while paused — as well as maliciously.
+
+The existing suite has `test_PauseBlocksDeposit` and `test_UnpauseRestoresDeposit`.
+Neither asks whether a depositor can get out.
+
+### Evidence
+
+Both lines are from the F3 run above:
+
+```
+[FAIL: EnforcedPause()] test_UsersCanExitWhilePaused() (gas: 239450)
+[FAIL: vault is paused forever with no owner left to unpause it] test_PauseCannotBeMadePermanent() (gas: 243001)
+```
+
+`test_UsersCanExitWhilePaused` deposits 10,000 USDT, pauses, and attempts a
+withdrawal of the depositor's own shares: `EnforcedPause()`.
+`test_PauseCannotBeMadePermanent` pauses, renounces ownership, and asserts the
+vault is not stuck — `vault.owner()` is `address(0)` and `paused()` is still true.
+
+### Fix
+
+* Remove `whenNotPaused` from `withdraw`. Withdrawals should be the one thing that
+  always works. If you need to stop an in-progress exploit draining the vault,
+  that is what a separate, time-boxed emergency mode is for — not the same flag
+  that gates deposits.
+* `override renounceOwnership()` to `revert()`, or replace `Ownable` with
+  `Ownable2Step` behind a multisig.
+* If you keep a pausable withdraw path for any reason, add a deadman switch: after
+  N days paused, withdrawals unblock automatically regardless of owner action.
+
+---
+
+## F5 — MEDIUM — `setDepositFee(10001)` bricks deposits with an unnamed panic.
+
+No bound check means the owner can set a fee above 100%. `amount - fee` at line 88
+then underflows and every deposit reverts with `Panic(0x11)` — not a named error,
+so integrators see an unexplained failure. Deposits stay dead until the owner
+notices. At exactly `10000` the failure mode differs: `netAmount` is 0, `shares`
+is 0, and the revert is `NoSharesMinted()`.
+
+### Evidence
+
+From the F3 run:
+
+```
+[FAIL: panic: arithmetic underflow or overflow (0x11)] test_DepositFeeCannotExceedOneHundredPercent() (gas: 44794)
+```
+
+### Fix
+
+The bound from F3 covers this too. `require(newFeeBps <= MAX_DEPOSIT_FEE_BPS)`
+with a sane constant makes both the underflow and the 100%-fee case unreachable.
+
+---
+
+## F6 — LOW/MEDIUM — the 30 bps withdrawal fee can be rounded away entirely.
+
+`fee = (grossAssets * 30) / 10_000` floors. For any `grossAssets < 334` base units
+the fee is exactly 0. Shares carry 18 decimals against a 6-decimal asset, so a
+depositor can name a share amount worth 333 base units with full precision and
+repeat. Exiting in slices costs gas but pays no fee.
+
+Whether this matters depends on whether the fee is load-bearing. Right now it is
+the only thing that accrues to remaining holders, so it is.
+
+### Evidence
+
+From the F3 run:
+
+```
+[FAIL: withdrawal fee was avoided entirely: 66600 != 66401] test_WithdrawalFeeCannotBeRoundedAway() (gas: 3625241)
+Logs:
+  gross value of the 200 slices: 66600
+  actually paid out            : 66600
+```
+
+200 slices of 333 base units: 66,600 gross, 66,600 paid out, 0 fee. The expected
+figure with the fee applied is 66,401.
+
+### Fix
+
+Round the fee up rather than down: `grossAssets.mulDiv(WITHDRAW_FEE_BPS,
+BPS_DENOMINATOR, Math.Rounding.Ceil)`. `Math` is already imported. The general
+rule — round every fee in the protocol's favour and every user-facing quantity
+against the user — should be applied to `convertToShares`/`convertToAssets` too,
+and stated in the NatSpec so the next person does not "simplify" it back.
+
+---
+
+## F7 — LOW/MEDIUM — dust withdrawals burn shares and pay out zero.
+
+`convertToAssets` floors. A share amount worth less than one base unit produces
+`grossAssets == 0`, so `netAssets == 0` — but the shares are burned anyway, and
+`aavePool.withdraw(usdt, 0, msg.sender)` is still called. There is no
+`if (grossAssets == 0) revert` guard.
+
+Two distinct problems:
+
+* Against the mocks, the shares are destroyed for nothing.
+* Against **real Aave V3**, `withdraw` with `amount == 0` reverts with
+  `INVALID_AMOUNT` (error 26), so on mainnet this is a confusing hard revert
+  rather than a silent loss. Either way it is wrong; they just fail differently,
+  which is its own reason to fix it.
+
+### Evidence
+
+From the F3 run:
+
+```
+[FAIL: shares were burned and nothing was paid out: 0 <= 0] test_WithdrawNeverBurnsSharesForNothing() (gas: 254558)
+Logs:
+  shares burned: 999999999999
+  USDT received: 0
+```
+
+999,999,999,999 shares burned. Zero USDT out.
+
+### Fix
+
+Guard both ends of `withdraw`:
+
+```solidity
+uint256 grossAssets = convertToAssets(shares);
+if (grossAssets == 0) revert ZeroAssets();
+...
+if (netAssets == 0) revert ZeroAssets();
+```
+
+---
+
+## F8 — MEDIUM, conditional — `deposit` breaks if Tether re-enables its transfer fee.
+
+`deposit` assumes the vault receives exactly `amount`:
+
+```solidity
+usdt.safeTransferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), amount);
+usdt.approve(address(aavePool), amount);
+aavePool.supply(address(usdt), amount, address(this), AAVE_REFERRAL_CODE);
+totalAssetsStored += amount;
+```
+
+The deployed TetherToken has `basisPointsRate` and `maximumFee`, applied inside
+`transfer`/`transferFrom`, settable by Tether's owner through
+`setParams(uint256,uint256)` up to 20 bps. They read zero today:
+
+```console
+$ cast call 0xdAC17F958D2ee523a2206206994597C13D831ec7 "basisPointsRate()(uint256)" \
+    --rpc-url https://ethereum-rpc.publicnode.com
+0
+$ cast call 0xdAC17F958D2ee523a2206206994597C13D831ec7 "maximumFee()(uint256)" \
+    --rpc-url https://ethereum-rpc.publicnode.com
+0
+```
+
+Zero today, and the switch is one transaction away, controlled by a party you do
+not control. If it flips, the vault receives `amount - fee` but tries to supply
+`amount`, and books `amount`. Deposits revert outright once the shortfall exceeds
+the idle balance; before that, the books overstate the position.
+
+I am flagging this as conditional, not live. It is the cheapest of the eight to
+fix and the most annoying to discover in production.
+
+### Evidence
+
+```console
+$ forge test --match-path 'test/replica/UsdtReplica.t.sol' \
+    --match-test test_BooksMatchAaveWhenTetherChargesATransferFee -vv
+No files changed, compilation skipped
+
+Ran 1 test for test/replica/UsdtReplica.t.sol:UsdtReplicaTest
+[FAIL: vault cannot supply the amount it books: 999000000 < 1000000000] test_BooksMatchAaveWhenTetherChargesATransferFee() (gas: 121411)
+Logs:
+  vault asked to supply (amount): 1000000000
+  vault actually received       : 999000000
+  shortfall per deposit         : 1000000
+
+Suite result: FAILED. 0 passed; 1 failed; 0 skipped; finished in 660.12µs (89.77µs CPU time)
+
+Ran 1 test suite in 11.40ms (660.12µs CPU time): 0 tests passed, 1 failed, 0 skipped (1 total tests)
+
+Failing tests:
+Encountered 1 failing test in test/replica/UsdtReplica.t.sol:UsdtReplicaTest
+[FAIL: vault cannot supply the amount it books: 999000000 < 1000000000] test_BooksMatchAaveWhenTetherChargesATransferFee() (gas: 121411)
+
+Encountered a total of 1 failing tests, 0 tests succeeded
+
+Tip: Run `forge test --rerun` to retry only the 1 failed test
+```
+
+At 10 bps the vault receives 999.00 USDT against a booked 1,000.00 — 1.00 USDT
+short on every deposit.
+
+### Fix
+
+Measure, do not assume:
+
+```solidity
+uint256 before = usdt.balanceOf(address(this));
+usdt.safeTransferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), amount);
+uint256 received = usdt.balanceOf(address(this)) - before;
+```
+
+then use `received` for the approval, the `supply`, the share maths and the
+accounting. This is correct whether or not the fee is ever switched on.
+
+---
+
+## 9 — What came back clean
+
+This part matters as much as the findings, so here it is explicitly.
+
+I ran a stateful invariant suite — three actors calling `deposit`, `withdraw` and
+`setDepositFee` (bounded 0–10,000 bps) in arbitrary interleavings, 256 runs x
+500 calls = **128,000 calls per invariant** — against four properties:
+
+* `invariant_Solvent` — the vault's aUSDT balance always covers its own books
+* `invariant_ClaimsFitAssets` — shareholders' combined claims never exceed
+  `totalAssetsStored`
+* `invariant_NoValueCreation` — total USDT out never exceeds total USDT in
+* `invariant_SharePriceSane` — share price never collapses to zero while shares exist
