+All four hold. Concretely, that means:
+
+* **The share maths is not exploitable for theft between depositors.** Every
+  conversion uses `Math.mulDiv` with floor rounding in a consistent direction; I
+  could not construct a sequence where one actor extracts another's principal.
+* **There is no first-depositor inflation attack.** Because `totalAssetsStored` is
+  an internal counter rather than a token balance, a direct donation of USDT or
+  aUSDT to the vault does not move the share price. This is a genuinely good
+  property of the current design and you should be deliberate about preserving it
+  — the F2 fix removes it, which is why F2's write-up insists on virtual shares or
+  a dead first tranche as part of that change.
+* **`nonReentrant` is correctly placed** on both state-changing entry points, and
+  neither USDT nor aUSDT has a transfer hook, so there is no reentrancy path I can
+  find.
+
+I also want to be clear about what I have **not** covered, so you do not read more
+assurance into this than is there:
+
+* No review of Aave V3 operational risk — supply caps, a frozen or paused USDT
+  reserve, or a utilisation spike leaving insufficient liquidity for `withdraw`.
+  Each makes deposits or withdrawals revert; none is a bug in your code, but all
+  are things a vault holding real money should handle explicitly rather than by
+  reverting.
+* No gas or DoS analysis under adversarial block conditions.
+* No review of deployment scripts or constructor arguments — though note in
+  passing that the constructor validates none of its five addresses, so a zero or
+  transposed argument deploys silently.
+
+One design note that is *not* a finding, since README states it plainly: the
+deposit fee is never swept to the owner, so the protocol earns nothing, and for a
+sole depositor the deposit fee is a no-op (they own 100% of the shares, so they
+capture the fee they just paid). That is what the code does and what the README
+says it does. I mention it only because the F3 exploit is built out of exactly
+this mechanism, and because "fee" in the UI will mean something different from
+what most users assume.
+
+## Summary
+
+| # | Severity | Issue | Reproduced by |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| F1 | Critical | `deposit()` always reverts on real USDT (`approve` returns no data) | `test/fork/RealUsdtDeposit.t.sol`, `test/replica/UsdtReplica.t.sol` |
+| F2 | Critical | All Aave interest is stranded and unrecoverable; yield is always 0% | `test/fork/StrandedYield.t.sol` |
+| F3 | Critical | Owner can sandwich a deposit via unbounded `setDepositFee` and keep ~98% | `test/exploit/VaultExploits.t.sol` |
+| F4 | High | `pause()` blocks withdrawals; `renounceOwnership` makes it permanent | `test/exploit/VaultExploits.t.sol` |
+| F5 | Medium | `setDepositFee(>10000)` bricks deposits with an unnamed panic | `test/exploit/VaultExploits.t.sol` |
+| F6 | Low/Med | 30 bps withdrawal fee avoidable by slicing below the rounding floor | `test/exploit/VaultExploits.t.sol` |
+| F7 | Low/Med | Dust withdrawals burn shares for zero payout (hard revert on real Aave) | `test/exploit/VaultExploits.t.sol` |
+| F8 | Medium* | Fee-on-transfer USDT breaks accounting if Tether re-enables it | `test/replica/UsdtReplica.t.sol` |
+
+\* conditional on a Tether parameter change; zero today.
+
+The single most useful thing to take from this is not any individual finding. It
+is that **the test suite's two mocks encode the two assumptions that are false** —
+that USDT is a standard ERC20, and that an Aave position does not grow. No amount
+of additional coverage against those mocks would have found F1 or F2. Before this
+ships, the suite needs a mainnet-fork tier exercising the happy path against the
+real USDT and the real pool, and an interest-accruing pool mock for the offline
+tier.
